  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Rolls Royce obligated $798M in federal contracts in FY2026 to date. Rolls Royce received 280 awards so far this fiscal year, with an average award size of $2.8M. Rolls Royce obligations fell 40.8% year over year. Historical obligations show fiscal-year totals of $1B in FY21, $990M in FY22, $989M in FY23, $860M in FY24, and $1.3B in FY25. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, and Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing is the dominant NAICS market for Rolls Royce work. The current-year totals are partial, noted as fiscal year to date; award-value figures in largest-awards and expiring-contracts tables are total contract values, while the other figures are contract obligations. This profile is focused on obligations rather than total contract ceilings or potential future awards. Use the agency and NAICS breakdowns to identify where federal spend is concentrated and to prioritize capture and compliance resources against the largest ongoing sources of Rolls Royce federal revenue.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are Rolls Royce's top federal buyers?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $763M for Rolls Royce in FY2026 to date. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $34.3M. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $430K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What markets does Rolls Royce serve for federal work?",
        "body": "Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing accounted for $723M of Rolls Royce obligations in FY2026 to date. Other Support Activities for Air Transportation accounted for $34.2M, and Aircraft Manufacturing accounted for $17.8M. Rolls Royce also has smaller obligations in related manufacturing and engineering NAICS categories, including lines listed at $11.2M, $3.7M, $3.4M, $2M, $918K, $860K, and $111K."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Rolls Royce make from federal contracts?",
      "a": "Rolls Royce obligated $798M from federal contracts in FY2026 to date. Rolls Royce received 280 awards this fiscal year with an average award size of $2.8M, and obligations fell 40.8% year over year. Historical fiscal-year obligations were $1B in FY21, $990M in FY22, $989M in FY23, $860M in FY24, and $1.3B in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies buy from Rolls Royce?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense is the top buyer for Rolls Royce, obligated $763M in FY2026 to date. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $34.3M, and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $430K."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Rolls Royce sell to the government?",
      "a": "Rolls Royce sells primarily aircraft engines and engine parts to the government, with Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing obligations of $723M in FY2026 to date. Rolls Royce also provides services and products in Other Support Activities for Air Transportation at $34.2M, Aircraft Manufacturing at $17.8M, and several other manufacturing and services NAICS categories listed in the profile."
    },
